---
// "Library & publishing" — the reference group of /docs.
//
// Sourced from evopet-library/README.md and src/manifest.ts, and from the
// desktop app's own settings module for the auto-upload switch. No hosted
// deployment is claimed: the service is written and passes its round trip, and
// it is not deployed.
import { DOC_GROUPS, type DocGroup } from '../../data/nav';

const group = DOC_GROUPS.find((entry) => entry.id === 'library') as DocGroup;

const endpoints: [string, string, string, string][] = [
  ['GET', '/api/health', '—', 'liveness, storage mode, public base URL'],
  ['GET', '/api/desktop/library', 'bearer', 'the app’s library response'],
  ['POST', '/api/pets/publish', 'bearer', 'publish a package (multipart)'],
  ['GET', '/api/manifest', '—', 'the public index, approved pets only'],
  ['GET', '/api/pets/<slug>', '— / owner', 'the index record'],
  ['GET', '/api/pets/<slug>/pet.json', '— / owner', 'the published pet.json, verbatim'],
  ['GET', '/api/pets/<slug>/spritesheet.webp|png', '— / owner', 'the published atlas'],
  ['GET', '/api/pets/<slug>/thumbnail.webp|png', '— / owner', 'the preview image'],
  ['POST', '/api/pets/<slug>/catch', 'bearer', 'record “downloaded from the catalogue”'],
  ['POST', '/api/admin/pets/<slug>/status', 'operator', 'set pending | approved | rejected'],
  ['DELETE', '/api/pets/<slug>', 'owner / operator', 'remove a package, bytes included'],
  ['GET', '/api/admin/deletions', 'operator', 'the deletion audit log'],
];

const transitions: [string, string, string][] = [
  ['—', 'approved', 'publish, no flag (the default)'],
  ['—', 'pending', 'publish with public=false'],
  ['pending', 'approved', 'owner re-publishing, or an operator'],
  ['approved', 'pending', 'owner re-publishing with public=false, or an operator (unpublish)'],
  ['pending / approved', 'rejected', 'operator'],
  ['rejected', 'pending / approved', 'owner re-publishing, or an operator'],
  ['any', 'gone', 'DELETE, owner (own pet) or operator (any pet)'],
];

const issueKey = `npm run key:issue -- --email you@example.com --username you --display "owner key"
npm run key:revoke -- --id <id>`;

const publishCurl = `curl -X POST "https://<host>/api/pets/publish" \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer <key>" \\
  -F "pet.json=@pet.json" \\
  -F "spritesheet.webp=@spritesheet.webp" \\
  -F "thumbnail.png=@thumbnail.png" \\
  -F "public=true"`;

const keychainBlob = `security add-generic-password -U -s dev.petdex.desktop-native -a oauth \\
  -w '{"access_token":"<key>","refresh_token":""}'
PETDEX_LIBRARY_URL=https://<host>/api/desktop/library`;

const deleteCurl = `curl -X DELETE "https://<host>/api/pets/<slug>?reason=<complaint%20ref>" \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer <operator key>"`;
---

<section class="docs-group" id={group.id}>
  <h2>{group.label}</h2>
  <p class="lede">{group.summary}</p>

  <div class="note note--honest">
    <span class="note-title">Not deployed</span>
    <p>
      The library service is written and passes its own end-to-end round trip, but it is not
      deployed. The base URL the site calls is a placeholder until a host is chosen, which must
      happen <em>before</em> the first publish: every URL in the index is absolute and issued from
      that value. Until then the library page reports that it cannot reach the library.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="docs-section" id="endpoints">
    <h3>Endpoints</h3>
    <p>
      One service, no database, no auth provider: Node and TypeScript, deployed as a single function,
      with one runtime dependency (the platform's own blob client). Public and owner-only bytes are
      separated deliberately: an <code>approved</code> pet's files go to anyone, a{' '}
      <code>pending</code> or <code>rejected</code> one's only to its owner or an operator, and an
      unauthorised request gets <code>404</code> rather than <code>403</code> so a slug cannot probe
      for hidden pets.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">method</th>
            <th scope="col">path</th>
            <th scope="col">auth</th>
            <th scope="col">purpose</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            endpoints.map(([method, path, auth, purpose]) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row" class="num">{method}</th>
                <td><code>{path}</code></td>
                <td>{auth}</td>
                <td>{purpose}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The index the library page reads is <code>GET /api/manifest</code>: public, unauthenticated,
      approved pets only, sorted by slug, and carrying <code>generatedAt</code>,{' '}
      <code>total</code>, <code>capableTotal</code>, <code>assetBase</code> and a row per pet with{' '}
      <code>slug</code>, <code>displayName</code>, <code>thumbnailUrl</code>,{' '}
      <code>capable</code>, <code>evopet</code>, <code>petJsonUrl</code>,{' '}
      <code>spritesheetUrl</code>, <code>version</code> and <code>updatedAt</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section" id="capability">
    <h3>Capability</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Evolution capability is the <code>evopet</code> block, and nothing else.</strong> An
      object-valued <code>evopet</code> key makes the pet capable: the parsed block is stored with
      the record and served in the index. Otherwise the package is stock and <code>evopet</code> is
      <code>null</code>. The service refuses <code>"evopet": "yes"</code> and checks its own flag
      against the app's scanner before storing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Declared <code>evolutionGates</code> are validated like <code>levels.validate_gates</code>:{' '}
      <span class="num">1</span>–<span class="num">4</span> gates, levels 1 to 99, strictly
      increasing. The index serves both the boolean and the block on purpose: the block is the whole
      truth, the boolean the convenience, so a client can always check the answer.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section" id="package">
    <h3>Package requirements</h3>
    <p>
      <code>POST /api/pets/publish</code> takes <code>multipart/form-data</code> only:
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">part</th><th scope="col">requirement</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><code>pet.json</code></th>
            <td>≤ 16 KB — the window the app reads capability from</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><code>spritesheet.webp</code> | <code>spritesheet.png</code></th>
            <td>exactly 1536×1872: 8 × 9 cells of 192×208, the atlas the app's sprite loader and EvoPet's compiler share</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><code>thumbnail.png</code> | <code>thumbnail.webp</code></th>
            <td>optional; ≤ 256 KB and ≤ 512×512</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><code>public=true|false</code></th>
            <td>optional; the off switch described below</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Validation happens before anything is stored: the manifest must parse, <code>id</code> must be
      a legal slug (<code>[a-z0-9-]</code>, <span class="num">1</span>–<span class="num">64</span>{' '}
      bytes, no leading or trailing dash; it becomes a directory name under{' '}
      <code>~/.petdex/pets</code>), <code>displayName</code> must fit{' '}
      <span class="num">96</span> bytes, the sheet must be the documented atlas of the documented
      kind, and <code>spritesheetPath</code> must agree with the uploaded sheet.
    </p>
    <p>The response is a record of what landed, not a promise:</p>
    <pre><code>{'{slug, status, url, capable, evopet, thumbnailUrl, version, replaced, notes}'}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section" id="auth">
    <h3>Auth v1</h3>
    <p>
      One bearer key per user, minted by the operator CLI and stored hashed with scrypt. The secret
      is never written, never logged, and never returned again. Format{' '}
      <code>evk1.&lt;id&gt;.&lt;secret&gt;</code>: the id is the public lookup path, the secret is 32
      random bytes.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{issueKey}</code></pre>
    <p>Publishing with a key:</p>
    <pre><code>{publishCurl}</code></pre>
    <div class="note note--warn">
      <span class="note-title">The one thing that must not go wrong</span>
    <p>
        The desktop app stores its session in the login keychain as a JSON blob, and{' '}
        <strong><code>refresh_token</code> must be empty</strong>. On a 401 the app posts a
        non-empty <code>refresh_token</code> to a third party's token endpoint, so a library key in
        the refresh slot leaks a credential. Write the blob, which is what <code>key:issue</code>{' '}
        prints:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{keychainBlob}</code></pre>
    <p>
        With <code>refresh_token</code> empty, a 401 surfaces as a sync error and nothing leaves the
        machine.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section" id="publication">
    <h3>Publication policy</h3>
    <p>
      Publishing is auto-upload: with no flag a package appears in the index immediately with the
      status the app already understands for a listed pet, <code>approved</code>. The off switch,{' '}
      <code>public=false</code>, is the only thing that produces <code>pending</code>, and the only
      setting in the system that persists a choice: <code>~/.evopet/settings.json</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <caption>The auto-upload switch, as the app implements it.</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">item</th><th scope="col">value</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">file</th><td><code>~/.evopet/settings.json</code></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">document</th><td><code>{'{"autoUpload": true}'}</code> or <code>{'{"autoUpload": false}'}</code>, written whole on every change</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">default</th><td>on. Only an explicit <code>false</code> turns publishing off: a missing key, a missing file, an unreadable file or a value that is not a bare boolean all resolve to the default</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">where it is set</th><td>the desktop app: Settings → Pet library</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">copy in the app</th><td>“A newly hatched pet is published to the EvoPet library so others can find it; turn this off to keep every pet local”</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Both statuses already exist in the app, and the off switch needs somewhere honest to leave an
      unlisted package. The default path produces neither; rejection is a status change, not a queue.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">from</th>
            <th scope="col">to</th>
            <th scope="col">by</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            transitions.map(([from, to, by]) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{from}</th>
                <td>{to}</td>
                <td>{by}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The catalogue is stored <strong>one object per pet</strong>, not one document. A single
      document would be a read-modify-write with no compare-and-swap behind it, so concurrent
      publishes could silently lose a pet. Per-pet keys make a publish one write to a key nobody else
      is writing; listing is then a list plus N reads.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section" id="deletion">
    <h3>Deletion and takedown</h3>
    <p>
      <code>DELETE /api/pets/&lt;slug&gt;</code> removes the index record and every package object;
      no tombstone serves anything afterwards, and the slug becomes a <code>404</code>. An owner can
      delete their own pet, and an operator key can delete <strong>any</strong> pet, optionally with
      a reason kept in the audit log.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{deleteCurl}</code></pre>
    <div class="note note--warn">
      <span class="note-title">No monitored takedown address exists yet</span>
    <p>
        A third-party complaint needs an address a rights holder can write to, and there is not one:
        removal is a manual operator action. Hosted public use should add a monitored address, a
        response-time commitment, and a repeat-infringer rule for publishing keys; this site makes no
        claim about how fast a takedown would be answered.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section" id="not-here">
    <h3>What this site does not do</h3>
    <ul class="prose">
      <li>
        <strong>No authenticated flow of any kind.</strong> No accounts, no OAuth, no keys, no
        uploads; the library page reads one public endpoint.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No hosted claim.</strong> The service's deploy steps live in its own repository; this
        site neither performs nor asserts a deployment, and does not choose the public base URL.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No install path that does not exist.</strong> The app reads its catalogue manifest
        from a compiled constant and trusts asset URLs under its own host, so{' '}
        <code>petdex://&lt;slug&gt;</code> installs still resolve to petdex.dev; only the library URL
        is overridable.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <p class="sources">
    <strong>Source.</strong> {group.source}; <code>evopet_settings.zig</code> and{' '}
    <code>settings_view.zig</code> for the auto-upload switch, quoted from its own copy.
  </p>
</section>
